# Shared TraceScene geometry tasks

Source: approved consolidation decision in the conversation. Upgrade TraceScene to serve the existing NearField tracing consumers, then remove the separate NearField geometry capture and GPU volumes. Preserve surface-cache capture/relighting, consumer-owned lighting algorithms, and WorldPartition residency ownership. World probes retain their existing scheduler.

## 1. Shared geometry and coverage contract

- [x] Inventory active geometry, lighting and material consumers; define a shared payload and sampling API covering known air, opaque geometry, unsupported geometry, unpublished/stale cells, material/emission and decoded light.
- [x] Define shared coverage and budgets: preserve the required 48-block near-field window and surface-cache tracing needs, share overlapping data, and justify which TraceScene levels are retained. Record formats, memory/upload costs and ownership before implementation.

Gate: every retained consumer has explicit data, coverage and validity requirements; no missing data is interpreted as empty geometry.

Contract and review: [shared geometry contract](LumOn.TraceSceneGeometryContract.md). Second review and independent completion audit passed. Delegated coverage/cost formula verification passed 333,216 assertions; no runtime implementation or performance result is claimed. Subsequent items implement this contract.

## 2. Shared worker capture and GPU publication

- [x] Adapt TraceScene to reuse worker-thread bulk capture and coalesced source snapshots; share source versions/invalidation and publish geometry, light, material references and readiness coherently.
- [x] Preserve WorldPartition as the residency owner, overlapping ring contents, bounded capture/upload work and stale-completion rejection. Keep old consumer wiring operational until the shared backend is verified.

Gate: controlled tests cover edits, missing chunks, unsupported shapes, movement, teleports and reused slots; no main-thread voxel capture or duplicate reads for the same shared source revision.

Evidence and traceability: [shared backend verification](LumOn.TraceSceneSharedBackendVerification.md). Second review and independent completion audit passed. Final combined validation passed 244 tests including 89 GPU cases; the final cache follow-up passed all 29 shared-backend cases. Production build passed with zero warnings/errors. Existing consumer wiring remains unchanged until item 3.

## 3. Switch tracing consumers

- [x] Switch screen-probe geometry tracing, hit lighting and world-probe visibility checks to the shared TraceScene resources. Preserve integer/fractional coordinate handling and hit/clear/unavailable/budget-exhausted outcomes.
- [x] Adapt surface-cache material capture and relighting to the shared sampling contract. Incomplete traces must not become successful clear segments or automatic sky contributions; preserve existing lighting evaluation for valid samples.

Gate: GPU regressions cover sealed rooms, doorways, missing/unsupported data, exhausted budgets, volume exits, camera motion and large coordinates, plus surface-cache capture/relight behavior.

Traceability for item 3:
- Screen/visibility switching -> [contract](LumOn.TraceSceneGeometryContract.md), Consumer inventory, Shared payload, Sampling and tracing API, Coverage and ring addressing -> one shared owner, independent logical domains, integer/fractional coordinates, explicit trace outcomes and material readiness -> `SharedTraceSceneScreenTests`, `LumOnDirectWorldProbeVisibilityTests.SharedDoorwayEditsAndCameraMovement`, and existing NearField/direct-visibility GPU regressions.
- Surface switching -> same contract, Shared payload, Sampling and tracing API, Ownership and implementation acceptance -> shared material capture, unchanged valid-hit shading, no sky/weight for incomplete rays, stale-history invalidation and retryable pages -> `SharedTraceSceneSurfaceTests`, existing surface GPU regressions, and `LumonSceneRelightBatchScheduleTests`.
- Runtime delivery -> same contract, Ownership and implementation acceptance -> shared bindings and regenerated shader assets -> production build with SPIR-V enabled and current-source GPU receipts. Legacy implementation removal, complete diagnostic updates and measured costs remain item 4.

Second review found and corrected partial-batch retry starvation, logical-domain history invalidation, signed ring wrapping and missing explicit compute bindings. A bounded runtime traversal loop resolved a driver instruction limit; the complete GLSL visibility run passed 76 tests (`artifacts/shared-consumers/final-visibility-fixed.trx`). The user subsequently required SPIR-V execution rather than a GLSL workaround for stale binaries. Compute tests now require compiled assets, and normal test builds regenerate and copy them. Missing-output regeneration, shared-include invalidation and rejection of disabled SPIR-V builds have passed (`artifacts/shared-consumers/spirv-*-proof.txt`). The final normal-build run passed all 28 strict SPIR-V compute tests with no skips (`artifacts/shared-consumers/compute-spirv-final.trx`); focused independent source review found no remaining compute blocker. The existing shader build covers compute stages only; graphics verification uses the existing GLSL path. The user accepted deferring shader-cost work to finish this item; extending graphics SPIR-V remains separate work. The matched worktree comparison records the link-time regression in `artifacts/visibility-comparison/comparison.md`; no rendering-performance improvement is claimed. Final current-source screen verification passed 91/91 cases (89 GPU and two scheduling tests), with no skips (`artifacts/shared-consumers/final-screen-current.trx`). Backend verification passed 131 cases in `final-backend-regression.trx`; its three fixture failures were corrected and passed in `final-spirv-backend-corrections.trx` (14/14). Normal SPIR-V-enabled builds passed. The second review and independent completion audit confirmed both item 3 requirements and the GPU gate fully satisfied, with no remaining required evidence gaps. Item 4 retains legacy removal, diagnostic cleanup and resource/performance measurements.

## 4. Remove duplication and validate

- [ ] Remove replaced NearField capture, storage, publication ownership and obsolete TraceScene companion-payload plumbing. Update bindings, diagnostics and architecture records; retain the NearField Geometry viewer using the shared data.
- [ ] Run builds and relevant regressions through a validation subagent. Compare source captures, uploads, resident memory and frame-thread work against the pre-change baseline for startup, movement and edits.

Gate: one shared geometry pipeline serves the retained consumers, no obsolete backend remains active, and resource/performance differences are recorded without conflating requested coverage with readiness.

Scope boundary: this work does not implement surface-cache hit-lighting integration into probes, migrate world-probe tracing to the GPU, change world-probe scheduling, or add multibounce lighting. Those remain separate work.
